  "lead": "Each 200 m square gets 15 indicators, each scaled from 0 (poor) to 100 (good). A score is the weighted average of the indicators that are ticked. All inputs are free, open data; nothing is invented.",
  "validation": "The roe deer score is fitted and tested on real sightings: 155 GBIF records since 2017 (citizen science, location within 250 m). People report animals where people go, so the comparison is made against 1724 records of other mammals by the same kind of observers since 2017 (\"target-group background\"). AUC (area under the ROC curve; ROC = receiver operating characteristic) is the chance that a square with a roe deer record scores higher than a square with another mammal record: 0.5 = no better than chance, 0.7 or more = useful. Tested on 9 km blocks the fit never saw, the score reaches AUC 0.73 (0.53 against the whole region). Only records from 2017 on are used, so the test matches the Sentinel-2 era rather than decades-old observations.",
